  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Monday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows splitting trof moving slowly thru\nwestern/central MT. 300mb jet divergence continues to produce some\nlight showers which are moving west-to-east across southern\nportions of our forecast area. Drying/clearing has already\noccurred over our west, but given the trof axis is still to our\nwest there should be some mainly mountain showers develop in the\nlate afternoon and early evening. High res models seem consistent\nhere. Freezing levels support snow showers above 11.5kft, so the \nhighest peaks of the Beartooths could see a little wet snow. A\npotentially more impactful item to watch over the next few hours\nis whether or not any strong t-storms develop near the Dakotas\nborder. Mesoanalysis shows increasing (but very capped)\ninstability, with mlcapes exceeding 1000 j/kg in Fallon/Carter.\nHowever, a shift to much drier west winds is rapidly approaching \nand thus, all cams show stronger convection developing east of the\nMontana state line later this afternoon. In fact, SPC's day1 \noutlook shows a marginal risk of severe t-storms over the Black \nHills. Ascent with the upstream wave continues thru 00z, so this \nis something to watch.\n\nDownslope gradients tighten somewhat in our west tonight, so look\nfor developing breezy conditions along the western foothills. This\nwill translate to breezy conditions Tuesday with temps getting \nwell into the 70s.\n\nOtherwise, we are entering a drier/quieter weather pattern under\nW/NW flow aloft tomorrow thru the weekend. There will be a series\nof weak shortwaves tracking near the MT/Canada border that will\nallow for periodic modest pushes of cooler Canadian air\n(Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday into Saturday). It is possible\nthat light precip will be associated with some of these weak \nfrontal pushes, mainly north of the southern foothills, and this \nis a source of model uncertainty at this time. Temperatures are\nlikely to fluctuate between near normal and warmer than normal.\n\nOverwhelming ensemble consensus is for building heights by the end\nof the week and into next week, due to a strengthening ridge\nanchored to our west. This could keep us prone to periodic\nbackdoor \"cooling\" but the high height regime definitely favors\nabove normal temps and dry conditions moving into early next week.\n\nFor those looking for a dose of winter as we move into early\nOctober, that is not in the cards yet. There is high confidence\nfor above normal temps and below normal precip through the first\nthird of the month, per western CONUS ridging. Cooler air will be\nover the eastern half of the country. The Pacific tropics remain\nquite active and should be monitored as well.\n\nJKL",
